what did it feel like getting the group  back together and first of all what does  it feel like to actually talk to someone  i i just to talk to you we haven't seen  each other in way too long years we went  through so much press together just  before the pandemic and got to see each  other so much which was always such a  pleasure  so this is amazing  this is as radical as when i got to see  this amazing uh group of people together  both my original cast all of us together  from the first jurassic park  and then this larger cast of jurassic  world  you know because of the global pandemic  we all lived together for five months  if our world's gonna survive what  matters is what we do now  i could use your expertise  you also told me you did some of the adr  in your closet yes  in fact we all did  my  i will remember well my daughter on zoom  in ninth grade across the house  coming in freaked out terrified because  i was screaming bloody murder  because i was running from a  giganotosaurus but in my closet in the  house in the house  oh my god and when she finally gets in  there she's like mom really mom you're  gonna scare the neighbors i'm like i'm  sorry i'm making a dinosaur movie in my  closet  you have lived most of your adult life  in this franchise what has that been  like and  people don't realize that yeah it's been  amazing you know i mean what's been  incredible as a young actor  having a lot of different worlds that i  got to be part of world of working with  david lynch and many other directors  this first experience of meeting and  finding the genius of steven spielberg  would turn into a lifelong conversation  of families and young children coming up  to me  young women in in science and history  and politics now saying what dr ellie  sattler meant to them as  as a badass character as a feminist um  so how it has lived and how this  character has lived for so many of us  has become in fact a big part of my  whole life you played a woman with a  doctorate a man with a tenured professor  at the tender age of 23. yes and people  are like what  and to see how she would evolve yes even  even in the world of science  you know for this final uh jurassic  world film  um was a really amazing and delicious  collaboration to have with colin  trevarro our co-writer and director and  steven spielberg and frank marshall  um  because  you know she was radical and she was  radical at a time that as you know very  well  we did not see female characters like  this in action movies no and it was a  big deal to see this woman who was  kicking ass but she was also a professor  and she was on equals with the other  people the men that were there yeah and  even when they were dealing with some  hardship or injury she was like don't  you worry yourself i got it i'll go get  the power back on and by the way she's  still like that now just so everybody  knows she is still like that still like  that you get back  you're reunited with sam how was that  amazing i mean sam neil is the dream  come true as an actor as a friend  to go through hard times with um he's  just an extraordinary gentleman  and the minute  i walked at that dig site into a tent  and there was dr alan grant  still waiting  for me to show up  i was i was very moved and it was really  special and then one of my favorite  memories of the whole experience was  the first day that sam neil and jeff  goldblum and i had a scene together  him with his ian malcolm with his  leather jacket and alan grant with his  hat we got into a jeep and as we got in  position we looked out and the crew was  surrounding the jeep and teary-eyed  because they were seeing these  characters that they had loved from the  first film kind of come back together  and i think this franchise has meant a  lot to people growing up and  i think colin really wanted to bring the  nostalgia of the first film back as well  i also think that people were waiting  for the romance between you and sam they  were so excited what was that was that  like the kiss  i mean  let's just see what happens  right don't give it away don't give it  away  but also it's this is a movie that's  helping bring people back to theaters  the comfort of theaters and what does  that mean and how important is that i  mean i know for me i was raised by  actors as you know  going to the movies has been  truly one of my great joys of my life  sharing with strangers  that you know collective experience of  storytelling is such a huge part of my  life  so for us a group of us who made the  movie only a couple weeks ago sitting  for the first time in a theater  watching the spectacle of this movie  in amazing right  screaming laughing cheering for each  other  cheering when we would do something  crazy laughing so hard at certain lines  i just felt so incredible you know  because we're fans and a lot of the  dinosaur sequences we hadn't seen just  like when we saw the first movie  so that was an amazing thing that we  hope  families and people who love this  franchise will really enjoy what was it  like integrating the new school crew  because there is a whole new generation  coming up now  amazing yeah how were they well first of  all as a woman  who with an amazing group of  collaborators as we talked about on the  first movie  we were really fighting for  something  new for a female character  to then see the franchise continue with  bryce dallas howard and now be joined  with the amazing dewanda wise  who is so great  and  live with she and her amazing husband  alano and  become family  who really like adopted my teenage  daughter who was there with us  was incredible and she's so amazing and  mamadou who's so amazing the film um the  amazing campbell scott i mean such great  new actors joining us  is this the end is this the end of the  line for your character  well  dinosaurs are always in all our hearts  and i would say it could never be the  end of the potential  of where this world could go  and  you know  seemingly it's um  there's a conclusion to many of the  stories that have been left unanswered  between jurassic park and jurassic world  but no one could have told me i was ever  going to play dr ellie sattler again  when we made this movie in 93. so i  ain't saying anything that's true right  you never know you never know where  ellie will end up because you're right  you're right yeah and i feel like also  she's a part of she's like a fixture for  certain things there there are people  who it does the movies feel weird  without you right  well i'm so happy she came back and she  came back to  really focus on preventing another  extinction that dinosaurs have a lot to  tell us about  and  hopefully the film really holds that  messaging  that we need to learn to peacefully  co-exist which we can take that on a lot  of levels where we need advancement and  compassion  hey before we go real quick what do your  kids think of this this branch because  they've grown up in this franchise what  do they think of it you know my daughter  had never seen it till right before we  started this movie  my son had seen it because he was a  little bit older because seeing  dinosaurs is scary but seeing them chase  your mother is like a whole nother thing  right so  so you have to pick the timing on that  but they're coming with me our world  premiere is here in los angeles and  they're coming with me and  so excited to get to see it  um  you know and the families of you know  our 400 crew members a lot of us had to  be away from our families to keep each  other safe so we really feel like it's a  community of our families helping us  sacrifice a lot to make this movie as  they were you know in  lockdown zoom school and all of that so  it's a shared experience that we're all  going to have together with our families  [Music]

